  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA low pressure system, will continue to cross the forecast area\nthrough the the day on Thursday. Showers are expected with\namounts between 0.30\" and 0.50\" for rainfall. The front is\nexpected to pass through by 04Z Friday morning allowing the \nrainfall to change to snow showers. \n\nSnow showers are then expected Thursday night and through the\nday on Friday and into the weekend. A slightly southern and\nfaster storm track has lowered snow totals a bit. Thus, expect\nless than advisory criteria for the WV mountains. In fact, the\nprobs of exceeding 3 inches has dropped to 10% to 20%. \n\nNarrow bands of lake-enhanced snow showers may develop Friday\ninto Saturday between Cleveland, potentially resulting in minor\naccumulations on grassy surfaces. However, the low-to-mid level\nflow off the Great Lakes is expected to be weak, limiting the\npotential for prolonged and intense lake-effect snow. At the\nmoment, Tucker County remains at the border for Advisory level\nsnow. A higher-end scenario, with localized snowfall totals of\n4-5 inches in eastern Tucker would require persistent, training\nsnow showers to develop. \n\nWith a building ridge over the Midwest and Great Lakes Sunday,\nthe probability of snow will gradually decrease throughout the\nday. Dry and quiet weather are expected Sunday night into \nWednesday.",
